      "Checking Customer Identity",
      "The USA PATRIOT Act requires firms to use a customer's name, address, date of birth, and Social Security or tax ID number to independently verify who they are. This can be done with a government-issued photo ID, such as:\n• A current driver's license\n• A passport\n• An alien registration card\n• A green card\n• Another government-issued ID (a Social Security card is not acceptable)\n\nVerification must be done within a reasonable time after opening the account.\n\nUnder the Customer Identification Program (CIP), firms must also check whether the person is on the list of known or suspected terrorists kept by the Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). This is the Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons (SDN) list. These rules help find and stop money laundering and terrorist funding. Identity verification records must be kept for at least 5 years.\n\nThe Know Your Customer (KYC) rule requires firms to use reasonable diligence to collect and keep essential information about customers. Firms check customers' identities, investment histories, investment objectives, and sources of funds. This helps stop money laundering and supports suitable recommendations.\n\nIf some information is missing, the account may still open if the firm has the essential facts needed to:\n• Service the account\n• Carry out special handling instructions\n• Know who has trading authority\n• Follow securities laws and rules\n\nWhen information is missing, a supervisor must be told, and the reasons must be asked about and documented. KYC also requires regular contact to see if objectives or facts have changed. The SEC requires suitability information to be updated at least every 3 years, but KYC may require it more often.",
